    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper argues that the K-Pg mass extinction was a low-probability event (~13%), as it required the asteroid to strike a hydrocarbon-rich area, which uniquely produced enough stratospheric soot to cause the extreme global cooling necessary to trigger the event and alter life's history.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Chicxulub asteroid impact",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Yucatan Peninsula",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Sixty-six million years ago, an asteroid approximately 9 km in diameter hit the hydrocarbon- and sulfur-rich sedimentary rocks in what is now Mexico. Recent studies have shown that this impact at the Yucatan Peninsula heated the hydrocarbon and sulfur in these rocks, forming stratospheric soot and sulfate aerosols and causing extreme global cooling and drought.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Chicxulub asteroid impact",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "asteroid impact",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Sixty-six million years ago, the Chicxulub asteroid impact in what is now Mexico led to ecosystem collapse including devastation of land vegetation\u00b9, the extinction of dinosaurs and >75% of all land and sea animals, and the subsequent macroevolution of mammals\u00b9\u207b\u2075. The coincidence of a mass extinction at the Cretaceous/Paleogene (K\u2212Pg) boundary and the iridium (sourced from the asteroid) layer of the Chicxulub impact were demonstrated using marine microfossils and fossil pollen \u2076\u207b\u2078. All available evidence suggests that the Chicxulub impact was the driver of the extinction.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "mass extinction",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "mass extinction",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "These events triggered a mass extinction, including dinosaurs, and led to the subsequent macroevolution of mammals. The amount of hydrocarbon and sulfur in rocks varies widely, depending on location, which suggests that cooling and extinction levels were dependent on impact site. Here we show that the probability of significant global cooling, mass extinction, and the subsequent appearance of mammals was quite low after an asteroid impact on the Earth's surface.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MRI-CGCM3",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "control simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "We also used model calculation results for the 500-Tg, 1500-Tg, and 2600-Tg BC cases and a 30-year control experiment with no BC ejection\u00b9. In these cases, BC was ejected into one column of the model grid box at 21\u00b0N, 90\u00b0W (Yucatan Peninsula) in the current geographical setting using the vertical distribution obtained by Saito et al.33.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MRI-CGCM3",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Optical Properties of Aerosols and Clouds",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "In these calculations, we assumed spherical particles for atmospheric aerosols and their optical properties in the solar and terrestrial spectral range were calculated on the basis of microphysical data such as the size distribution and spectra refractive index using the software package Optical Properties of Aerosols and Clouds (OPAC)34.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MRI-CGCM3",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "spherical particles for atmospheric aerosols",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "In these calculations, we assumed spherical particles for atmospheric aerosols and their optical properties in the solar and terrestrial spectral range were calculated on the basis of microphysical data such as the size distribution and spectra refractive index using the software package Optical Properties of Aerosols and Clouds (OPAC)34.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "asteroid impact",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Popigai crater",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "We also performed an additional 10-year experiment for the high-latitude 200-Tg BC ejection case, where BC was ejected at the late-Eocene Popigai crater (71\u00b0N, 111\u00b0E); all other conditions were the same as those for the Chicxulub cases.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
